1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,300
Your agent just ran fourteen steps.

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:07,650
Then it deleted your tests
and overwrote your config.

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:10,800
Can you get them back?

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,950
Three parts: the problem,
how it works, the fix.

5
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:19,850
Why can't git help?
The third test was never committed.

6
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:23,600
Claude Code's docs:
shell-only changes aren't tracked.

7
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:27,550
So what would help?
Think of a flight recorder.

8
00:00:27,550 --> 00:00:31,800
It keeps every move, so you can replay it.

9
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,350
So how does rewindagents
record your agent?

10
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:39,450
Every tool call goes into
one hash-chained log.

11
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:43,900
Before each edit or shell command,
files are saved.

12
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:49,400
So which step would you rewind to?

13
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:52,600
Twelve, because thirteen is the delete.

14
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:56,850
One command, and four files come back.

15
00:00:56,850 --> 00:01:01,350
Even the rewind can be undone.

16
00:01:01,350 --> 00:01:05,850
Where does the analogy break?
Recorders can't undo a crash.

17
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:09,800
Unlike a recorder,
this one puts your files back.

18
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:14,050
Preview with diff.
Take back one file with --only.

19
00:01:14,050 --> 00:01:19,200
You can now see every step
and the change it made.

20
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:24,200
Apache-2.0. Standard library only.
Not on PyPI yet.

21
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:30,000
Your editor has had undo for decades.
Now your agent does.
